{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019GCUsehhN3ai4PPanjJx6r","type":"tool_result","content":"1004:[open_item.OI-32]\n1089:The owner directed the two be ruled as one, on the reading that a generated component view answers OI-33's maintenance-protocol question by deleting most of it. That reading is correct and OI-33's own recommendation had already reached it (\"if OI-32 rules the generated view, THIS BRIEF'S QUESTION LARGELY CEASES TO EXIST\"). OI-33 is ruled ABSORBED rather than deleted, so its id stays spent and its surviving residue is carried below rather than lost — that residue is PHASE 5 and it is the one obligation a generator cannot discharge for itself.\n1149:one_line = \"\"\"ABSORBED INTO OI-32, 2026-08-19 by owner direction - the two are ruled as one item. This row is NOT deleted: its id stays spent (D-4) and its surviving residue - the leave-the-requirement-rows-correct obligation and the LLR-037 measured miss - is carried as PHASE 5 of OI-32's combined brief. Nothing here is ruled on its own terms; rule OI-32. Original one-line follows. RE-POINTED 2026-08-18 by the owner's direction (NOT a ruling): if the component spec is GENERATED from the SRs and LLRs tied to the component, the maintenance protocol this row exists to design is answered by the generator - rule between a close ritual over a hand-maintained spec and a derived one that needs none\"\"\"\n1150:decision = \"\"\"OWNER DIRECTION, 2026-08-18 - A DIRECTION, NOT A RULING, and it re-points this row harder than it re-points OI-32.\n1156:WHY THIS RE-POINTS THIS ROW HARDER THAN OI-32. This brief was written to design a MAINTENANCE PROTOCOL for a hand-authored document, and its five obligations - (a) scope the work, (b) leave the spec at the right detail, (c) in the right location, (d) with superseded decisions REMOVED, (e) without restating the WI's own scope - are all obligations on a HUMAN OR AGENT AUTHOR. A generated view has none of them. (b) and (c) are the generator's layout. (d), named in this brief as the hardest of the five, DISSOLVES ENTIRELY: a superseded decision is removed from a generated view by amending the requirement row it came from, which is an act the spine already forces and already gates. (e) dissolves too - a generator cannot restate a work item's scope, because a work item's scope is not among its inputs.\n1180:DEPENDENCY, stated plainly so it is not discovered late: this is DOWNSTREAM OF OI-32. There is no location to write into until `docs/cmp/` exists. If OI-32 rules RETIRE, this item is moot and should be struck rather than left standing as a pending obligation against a home that will not be built.\"\"\"\n1192:- (e) DERIVE THE COMPONENT SPEC AND CARRY NO PROTOCOL AT ALL - the owner's direction, added 2026-08-18 and stated as a first-class option. The component's \"spec\" becomes a GENERATED view of the SRs and LLRs tied to it, regenerated and freshness-gated like every other derived artifact; the knowledge half becomes a derived function of the HATS that derived those rows rather than a hand-set `knowledge` cell. There is then no close ritual to design, because there is no hand-maintained document to leave correct. FOR: it dissolves (d), the hardest of this brief's five obligations - a superseded decision leaves a derived view when the requirement row it came from is amended, which is an act the spine already forces and already reviews, rather than an act depending on the closing author remembering that something was superseded at the one moment they are least motivated to write it down; it dissolves (e), the anti-restatement rule, because a generator has no access to a work item's scope statement and so cannot restate it - the failure mode this brief predicted (a component spec that becomes a changelog of work items) is UNREPRESENTABLE rather than forbidden; it adds no tax to any close, which is what makes prose-only rituals get skipped, and R-F's own admission is that the skipped party is the unattended loop; and its staleness is MECHANICALLY DETECTABLE by the same `--check` shape nine artifacts already use, where a prose spec's staleness is detectable by nothing, which is the honest ceiling this brief records on every other option. AGAINST: it moves the failure rather than removing it - a derived view is exactly as true as the rows it derives from, so a WI that closes leaving a stale LLR now produces a CONFIDENTLY WRONG generated page instead of an obviously old document, and this repo has a measured instance of precisely that (LLR-037 published a symbol deleted five weeks earlier, found by a census rather than by a check); it shows no internals, so the mechanism knowledge that lives in the five documents OI-32 would have promoted has no home under this option and needs its own disposition; and it needs attributes that do not exist yet, so unlike (a) it cannot start tomorrow.\"\"\"\n1195:THIS ROW IS STRICTLY DOWNSTREAM OF OI-32 AND THE DEPENDENCY IS NOW SHARPER THAN WHEN IT WAS WRITTEN. Before the direction, the dependency was locational: no protocol until there is a place to write. After it, the dependency is EXISTENTIAL: if OI-32 rules the generated view, THIS BRIEF'S QUESTION LARGELY CEASES TO EXIST, and this row should be re-scoped rather than ruled - re-scoped to the one obligation that survives, stated below.\n1213:SEQUENCING: this cannot execute before OI-32, and it should not be ruled in a way that presumes OI-32's answer. If OI-32 rules MATERIALIZE, rule this in the same sitting - a home with no maintenance protocol is an archived spec that has not been archived yet, and promoting 3,000 lines of design into an unmaintained directory is a step backwards from leaving it where it is. If OI-32 rules RETIRE, strike this row rather than leaving it pending against a destination that will not exist.\"\"\"\n1228:DEPENDENCY, EXPLICIT: THIS IS THE SAME THREAD AS OI-32 AND OI-33. The hat field is one half of the generated-component direction recorded on those two rows - \"SRs tagged with the hat that derived them\" is exactly the attribute OI-33's direction names, and the component view this ruling feeds is the one OI-32's direction proposes generating. Rule and execute them together; a hat field minted without the generated view is a cell with no consumer, and the generated view without the hat field can only carry the requirements half.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"633ad3f8-ab4a-4dc6-96b2-afc8f3e47b83","uuid":"f7993023-94ac-445c-b8f3-d3afd2330001","timestamp":"2026-08-30T17:22:19.447Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"1004:[open_item.OI-32]\n1089:The owner directed the two be ruled as one, on the reading that a generated component view answers OI-33's maintenance-protocol question by deleting most of it. That reading is correct and OI-33's own recommendation had already reached it (\"if OI-32 rules the generated view, THIS BRIEF'S QUESTION LARGELY CEASES TO EXIST\"). OI-33 is ruled ABSORBED rather than deleted, so its id stays spent and its surviving residue is carried below rather than lost — that residue is PHASE 5 and it is the one obligation a generator cannot discharge for itself.\n1149:one_line = \"\"\"ABSORBED INTO OI-32, 2026-08-19 by owner direction - the two are ruled as one item. This row is NOT deleted: its id stays spent (D-4) and its surviving residue - the leave-the-requirement-rows-correct obligation and the LLR-037 measured miss - is carried as PHASE 5 of OI-32's combined brief. Nothing here is ruled on its own terms; rule OI-32. Original one-line follows. RE-POINTED 2026-08-18 by the owner's direction (NOT a ruling): if the component spec is GENERATED from the SRs and LLRs tied to the component, the maintenance protocol this row exists to design is answered by the generator - rule between a close ritual over a hand-maintained spec and a derived one that needs none\"\"\"\n1150:decision = \"\"\"OWNER DIRECTION, 2026-08-18 - A DIRECTION, NOT A RULING, and it re-points t